1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:05,145
Look closely at a rock and you're looking
at a story millions of years long.

2
00:00:05,185 --> 00:00:09,715
Some rocks were born in fire,
from molten rock deep underground.

3
00:00:09,755 --> 00:00:13,745
Some were built grain by grain
at the bottom of ancient seas.

4
00:00:13,785 --> 00:00:18,651
And some were squeezed and baked until
they became something completely new.

5
00:00:18,691 --> 00:00:23,955
Today we'll read those stories: igneous,
sedimentary and metamorphic rock

6
00:00:23,995 --> 00:00:29,721
— how each one forms, how to recognise it,
and how one turns into another.

7
00:00:34,717 --> 00:00:40,202
Rocks belong to the lithosphere: the
crust plus the rigid top of the mantle.

8
00:00:40,242 --> 00:00:45,133
When the young Earth was extremely hot,
the heaviest materials sank to the

9
00:00:45,173 --> 00:00:50,280
centre and the lightest floated to
the top, so the planet formed layers.

10
00:00:50,320 --> 00:00:54,360
The crust we live on is thin
— about five to ten kilometres

11
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:58,929
under the oceans, and up to
about fifty under the continents.

12
00:00:58,969 --> 00:01:04,129
Below it lies the mantle, about two
thousand nine hundred kilometres thick,

13
00:01:04,169 --> 00:01:10,135
then the liquid outer core and the solid
inner core, both mostly iron and nickel.

14
00:01:10,950 --> 00:01:13,939
Now, two words that are easy to mix up.

15
00:01:13,979 --> 00:01:16,523
A mineral is a naturally occurring,

16
00:01:16,563 --> 00:01:20,860
non-living solid with a
definite chemical composition.

17
00:01:20,900 --> 00:01:24,943
Quartz, mica, feldspar
and gold are all minerals.

18
00:01:24,983 --> 00:01:28,763
A rock is a solid mixture
of one or more minerals.

19
00:01:28,803 --> 00:01:33,849
Think of the minerals as the ingredients,
and the rock as the baked cake.

20
00:01:33,889 --> 00:01:36,407
Geologists sort rocks into three types,

21
00:01:36,447 --> 00:01:40,339
and they group them by one
thing: how the rock formed.

22
00:01:41,150 --> 00:01:43,050
Igneous means 'fiery'.

23
00:01:43,090 --> 00:01:46,598
Igneous rocks form when
molten rock cools and hardens,

24
00:01:46,638 --> 00:01:50,824
and they were the first rocks
to form in the Earth's crust.

25
00:01:50,864 --> 00:01:54,564
It starts when a crack or
weakness opens in the crust.

26
00:01:54,604 --> 00:01:58,754
The pressure drops, and magma
— molten rock below the surface

27
00:01:58,794 --> 00:02:02,126
— pushes upwards along
the line of weakness.

28
00:02:02,166 --> 00:02:07,293
If the magma is forced in among other
rocks and cools inside the crust,

29
00:02:07,333 --> 00:02:11,500
it forms intrusive rock:
intrusive means 'forced in'.

30
00:02:11,540 --> 00:02:15,586
If it pours out onto the
surface as lava and cools there,

31
00:02:15,626 --> 00:02:18,700
it forms extrusive rock: 'forced out'.

32
00:02:19,483 --> 00:02:22,343
So how do you recognise igneous rock?

33
00:02:22,383 --> 00:02:26,248
First, it's crystalline:
as magma cools, silica,

34
00:02:26,288 --> 00:02:30,583
iron and magnesium combine
into mineral crystals.

35
00:02:30,623 --> 00:02:33,452
The speed of cooling decides their size.

36
00:02:33,492 --> 00:02:36,047
Deep underground, magma cools slowly,

37
00:02:36,087 --> 00:02:40,394
so the crystals have time to
grow large — that's granite.

38
00:02:40,434 --> 00:02:46,025
At the surface, lava cools quickly and
the crystals stay tiny — that's basalt.

39
00:02:46,065 --> 00:02:50,272
Igneous rock is also massive,
meaning it has no layers,

40
00:02:50,312 --> 00:02:56,337
and it contains no fossils, because
the heat destroys any living material.

41
00:02:56,377 --> 00:02:59,695
Granite, gabbro and
dolerite are intrusive.

42
00:02:59,735 --> 00:03:03,584
Basalt, pumice, obsidian
and scoria are extrusive —

43
00:03:03,624 --> 00:03:08,853
and pumice is so full of gas
bubbles that it can float on water.

44
00:03:09,650 --> 00:03:12,515
Sedimentary rocks are built from sediment,

45
00:03:12,555 --> 00:03:16,531
and the most common way is
mechanical, in four steps.

46
00:03:16,571 --> 00:03:18,411
One: erosion.

47
00:03:18,451 --> 00:03:22,732
Rivers, wind and ice wear
rock down into smaller pieces,

48
00:03:22,772 --> 00:03:26,014
bits of clay and dissolved substances.

49
00:03:26,054 --> 00:03:28,614
Two: transport and deposition.

50
00:03:28,654 --> 00:03:33,664
The pieces are carried away and dropped
at the bottom of a sea, lake or valley.

51
00:03:33,704 --> 00:03:38,729
Three: new layers settle on top of
older ones, over thousands of years.

52
00:03:38,769 --> 00:03:41,579
Four: compaction and cementation.

53
00:03:41,619 --> 00:03:46,135
The weight of the water and the layers
above squashes the lower layers,

54
00:03:46,175 --> 00:03:49,926
and dissolved minerals cement
the particles together.

55
00:03:50,717 --> 00:03:55,358
Sedimentary rock can also form
chemically, when water evaporates

56
00:03:55,398 --> 00:04:00,243
and leaves minerals behind,
like rock salt and some limestone.

57
00:04:00,283 --> 00:04:03,336
Or organically,
from the compressed remains of

58
00:04:03,376 --> 00:04:06,779
living things, like coal
and shelly limestone.

59
00:04:06,819 --> 00:04:11,519
You can spot sedimentary rock by
its visible layers, called strata.

60
00:04:11,559 --> 00:04:16,584
It's usually softer, and wears
away more easily than igneous rock.

61
00:04:16,624 --> 00:04:19,874
And it's the rock type
where fossils are found.

62
00:04:19,914 --> 00:04:22,471
Sandstone is made of sand grains,

63
00:04:22,511 --> 00:04:26,800
shale of clay and mud,
conglomerate of rounded pebbles,

64
00:04:26,840 --> 00:04:33,171
limestone of shells, coral and calcium
carbonate, and coal of plant remains.

65
00:04:34,083 --> 00:04:36,833
Metamorphic means 'changed in form'.

66
00:04:36,873 --> 00:04:39,200
These rocks start out as igneous,

67
00:04:39,240 --> 00:04:42,285
sedimentary or even
other metamorphic rock,

68
00:04:42,325 --> 00:04:48,086
and they're changed by heat, pressure,
or both — without melting completely.

69
00:04:48,126 --> 00:04:51,510
The heat comes from magma
pushing into the crust,

70
00:04:51,550 --> 00:04:55,284
sometimes as a huge
intrusion called a batholith.

71
00:04:55,324 --> 00:04:59,346
It makes the existing crystals
re-form as new crystals.

72
00:04:59,386 --> 00:05:04,388
The pressure comes from the weight of
the rock above, or from tectonic plates

73
00:05:04,428 --> 00:05:06,483
colliding and folding the crust,

74
00:05:06,523 --> 00:05:10,107
and it often squeezes the
new crystals into bands.

75
00:05:10,147 --> 00:05:13,657
Metamorphic rocks are usually
harder and denser than

76
00:05:13,697 --> 00:05:17,665
the rock they came from,
and they contain no fossils.

77
00:05:18,450 --> 00:05:20,580
Learn these five changes.

78
00:05:20,620 --> 00:05:22,400
Shale becomes slate.

79
00:05:22,440 --> 00:05:24,550
Limestone becomes marble.

80
00:05:24,590 --> 00:05:26,820
Sandstone becomes quartzite.

81
00:05:26,860 --> 00:05:28,720
Granite becomes gneiss.

82
00:05:28,760 --> 00:05:30,926
And coal becomes graphite.

83
00:05:30,966 --> 00:05:35,256
Two memory tricks: shale and
slate start with the same letter,

84
00:05:35,296 --> 00:05:39,696
and sand is mostly quartz,
so sandstone becomes quartzite.

85
00:05:40,583 --> 00:05:42,923
None of these rocks lasts forever.

86
00:05:42,963 --> 00:05:46,662
Over hundreds of millions
of years, rocks are melted,

87
00:05:46,702 --> 00:05:51,033
worn away, buried and changed,
then form new rocks again.

88
00:05:51,073 --> 00:05:54,786
This never-ending series of
changes is the rock cycle,

89
00:05:54,826 --> 00:05:57,569
and it has no beginning and no end.

90
00:05:57,609 --> 00:05:59,549
Let's follow one journey.

91
00:05:59,589 --> 00:06:03,030
Granite at the surface is
broken down where it lies —

92
00:06:03,070 --> 00:06:07,451
that's weathering — and the
pieces are carried off by erosion.

93
00:06:07,491 --> 00:06:12,879
The sand is deposited, then compacted
and cemented into sandstone.

94
00:06:12,919 --> 00:06:17,359
Buried deep, heat and pressure
turn the sandstone into quartzite.

95
00:06:17,399 --> 00:06:20,509
Pushed deeper still, it melts into magma.

96
00:06:20,549 --> 00:06:25,447
And when that magma cools slowly
underground, it forms new granite.

97
00:06:26,450 --> 00:06:28,670
Three traps that cost marks.

98
00:06:28,710 --> 00:06:32,284
One: weathering and erosion
are not the same thing.

99
00:06:32,324 --> 00:06:38,031
Weathering breaks rock down where it lies;
erosion wears it away and carries it off.

100
00:06:38,071 --> 00:06:40,791
Two: metamorphic rock doesn't melt.

101
00:06:40,831 --> 00:06:45,731
It changes while it's still solid
— if it melts, it becomes magma.

102
00:06:45,771 --> 00:06:49,665
Three: the rock cycle doesn't
start with igneous rock.

103
00:06:49,705 --> 00:06:54,405
Any rock can become any other type,
so there's no start and no end.

104
00:06:55,317 --> 00:06:56,677
Quick recap.

105
00:06:56,717 --> 00:07:03,495
Igneous rock forms when magma or lava
cools: crystals, no layers, no fossils.

106
00:07:03,535 --> 00:07:06,532
Sedimentary rock forms when sediment is

107
00:07:06,572 --> 00:07:10,309
compacted and cemented:
strata, and fossils.

108
00:07:10,349 --> 00:07:16,719
Metamorphic rock is changed by heat and
pressure: harder, denser, often banded.

109
00:07:17,069 --> 00:07:19,819
Your turn — pause if you need to.

110
00:07:19,859 --> 00:07:22,975
One: why does granite have large crystals?

111
00:07:23,015 --> 00:07:26,781
Two: what does limestone
become when it's metamorphosed?

112
00:07:26,821 --> 00:07:30,905
Three: which two processes
turn loose sand into sandstone?

113
00:07:35,955 --> 00:07:37,195
Answers!

114
00:07:37,235 --> 00:07:40,341
One: it cooled slowly, deep underground.

115
00:07:40,381 --> 00:07:41,911
Two: marble.

116
00:07:41,951 --> 00:07:44,681
Three: compaction and cementation.

117
00:07:44,721 --> 00:07:49,618
Get all three, and you're ready for
the rock cycle questions in your exam.
